        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"The laundromat had one machine nobody used.\\n\\nNumber nine.\\n\\nIts window was painted white from the inside, and someone had scratched DON'T COUNT into the coin slot.\\n\\nAt midnight, with every other washer occupied, I put my clothes in it.\\n\\nThe paint was gone now.\\n\\nI counted seven shirts, three pairs of trousers, eleven socks.\\n\\nTwenty-one things.\\n\\nThe machine started before I inserted any money.\\n\\nHalfway through the cycle, something knocked against the glass.\\n\\nA hand.\\n\\nI stepped back.\\n\\nIt was my hand. The little scar across the thumb, the crooked finger I'd broken at twelve.\\n\\nIt pressed against the window, then disappeared into the foam.\\n\\nThe attendant emerged from behind a curtain.\\n\\n“How many?” she asked.\\n\\nI couldn't answer.\\n\\n“How many did you put in?”\\n\\n“Twenty-one.”\\n\\nShe closed her eyes.\\n\\nInside the washer, somebody began counting.\\n\\nMy voice, muffled by water.\\n\\n“One. Two. Three.”\\n\\nThe attendant unplugged the machine.\\n\\nIt kept turning.\\n\\n“Listen carefully,” she said. “When it stops, take your clothes. Nothing else. Whatever you recognize.”\\n\\nThe counting reached twenty-one.\\n\\nThe door clicked open.\\n\\nMy clothes lay folded inside, perfectly dry.\\n\\nBeneath them was a photograph of my father holding a baby.\\n\\nOn the back, in his handwriting: Our second try.\\n\\nI had always been an only child.\\n\\nBehind the drum, someone whispered, “Twenty-two.”\\n\\nThe attendant caught my wrist before I reached in.\\n\\nOutside, I checked the bag.\\n\\nSeven shirts. Three trousers. Eleven socks.\\n\\nMy phone rang.\\n\\nDad.\\n\\nFor a long time he listened to me breathing.\\n\\nThen he asked, “Which one came out?”\"\n}",
